{"video_id": "2tYB_c9ULdA", "source_file": "How to Tell If the AI Bubble Is Real Without Trusting Anyone. [2tYB_c9ULdA].mp4", "source_sha256": "aa8d686833d537220b998f520cf60301423604f4efa4b5b2eebacdb694123763", "local_dur": 78.79, "model": "base", "engine": "openai-whisper", "status": "ASR_UNCERTIFIED", "device": "cpu", "elapsed_s": 8.5, "language": "en", "text": " Right now, half of the internet's screaming that AI is the biggest bubble in history. The other half's screaming that it's the biggest breakthrough since sliced bread, because the uncomfortable truth is that it doesn't matter which half of the internet is right. Both of them are watching the wrong thing entirely. They're missing what's hiding in plain sight. Everyone in this fight is arguing about the same thing. User growth, adoption, revenue, how many people are using this stuff. They're all just arguing about demand, but demand is also the side of the coin that can lie to you. It's noisy, it's narrative-driven, and it's a story that changes depending on who's telling it and what they're trying to sell you. But every coin has two sides. And the other side, the one nobody's talking about, is the side that paints a much better picture of what's actually unfolding. It's what the world's best investors have quietly used to track financial bubbles for more than 200 years. And it starts by looking at the other side of the coin. Supply. Because demand can be faked and will get more into exactly how that happens a bit later. But you can't fake supply. Supply takes real capital, committed up front, and it tells the real story of what's happening. This year alone, the biggest tech companies in the world are on track to spend around $725 billion on artificial intelligence. We've never seen a bubble of this size before. But as Mark Twain put it, history doesn't repeat itself, but it often rhymes. And what we're seeing right now is very familiar.", "segments": [{"id": 0, "seek": 0, "start": 0.0, "end": 4.08, "text":